{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZjn7wMHrFoS2Lphz1ry","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 13 is done and the gate passes: `lake build` ok, one `sorry` against a limit of one, 1,231 tests pass. It is marked `- [x]` in `iterations.md`, `PROGRESS.md` has its Iteration 13 section and now reads `Current: 13/14 SOLVED`, and nothing is committed. Item 14 is still open, so `LOOP_COMPLETE` was not added.\n\n**What was produced**\n- **`paper2/equivalences.md`** now opens as section 3:\n  - **Master table:** each of the twelve problems with its source, the relation actually proved and its hypothesis, the main Lean theorem, and a status from the five required labels (proved / sandwich / monotone only / stated gap / false as stated), plus a tally.\n  - **The chain:** the equivalence diagram (below).\n  - **Gaps and sorries:**\n    - The loop added no allowed sorries; the only `sorry` is the old conjecture in `Sandwich.lean`, and no section-3 theorem depends on it.\n    - The two monotonicity gaps (`NodeSearchMonotonicity`, `EdgeSearchMonotonicity`) are stated as `Prop`s and used only as hypotheses, never asserted.\n    - Errors found in the sources are listed, each with its Lean counterexample.\n\n  The earlier detailed table and the per-item sections stay below this as the full record.\n- **`paper2/figures/equivalence_chain.dot`**, rendered to `.pdf` and `.png`. Every edge is labelled with its Lean theorem. Solid edges are proved identities, dashed are proved inequalities, dotted are the two gaps, and red are the Table 1 claims that are false.\n- **`paper2/axiom_check.lean`** prints the axioms of all 33 theorems the section names. All 33 use only the three standard axioms. A control line on the conjecture shows `sorryAx`, which confirms the check can fail.\n- **`paper2/lean_repo_plan.md`** lists which files move to the paper's repository and in what order:\n  - 10 base files (1,392 lines);\n  - 11 `Complex/` files (6,022 lines);\n  - optionally, the 3 files with the pattern-graph counterexample;\n  - plus the Mathlib commit to pin, what stays behind, the checks to run, and the move steps.\n- `paper2/README.md`'s contents list now includes the new files.\n\n**The one finding with consequences for the move:** section 3 uses only five declarations from `Sandwich.lean`, all about bandwidth. Copying them into a new file of about 100 lines, `Bandwidth.lean`, removes the `Sandwich` import from the only two files that have it (`SplitBandwidth` and `EdgeSeparation`). I tested this with a throwaway build in `/tmp`, now deleted: both files build, their theorems keep the standard axioms, and the conjecture drops out of the imports. So the paper's repository can be built with no `sorry` at all.\n\n**Next:** item 14, the monotonicity theorem that would close both gaps."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1038,"cache_read_input_tokens":142280,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1038,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":785,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"747dc9e7-b51a-40c7-bf22-0f086073294d","uuid":"e1d79e15-d58f-4e02-9bc4-77fc002d150e","timestamp":"2026-09-30T13:55:35.667Z","request_id":"req_011CfZjn7VZNaL82ZeumK9J7"}
